Discussion of Wire-weave ROM electrical characteristics
With respect to over-current on wires.

#40 wire is 0.0031" diam, 1049 ohms/1000', "max amps for chassis wiring" 90 mA.

Wang ROM's have a 30 ohm series resitor on the 12V supply, passing through
two transistors (C-E junction) and one 74151 output to ground, plus each wire
which is maybe 3 feet in length - or about 3 ohms.  Without considering wire,
transistors, or logic output, the max current is 400 mA.  During normal
operation, this would be at 30% (27%) duty cycle, or 120 mA.  Since each
microcode cycle usually accesses a different wire, the duty cycle to a
given wire is likely much less than 30%. Some loops do briefly execute the
same instruction repeatedly, though. More common is every 3rd or 4th.

Loop current to given wire:

1	3/10	30%	120mA
2	3/20	15%	60mA
3	3/30	10%	40mA
4	3/40	7.5%	30mA

It is concievable that 1x loops might stress the wire enough to cause
concern. Unknown just how many loop iterations would cause concern,
though. Typical loops of this type would run 16 iterations (usually
dealing with a register).

There is, however, a loop on the Wang 720C:

564: V = V + 0 + 1; jump 564[KBD:]

This loop is entered, at least some times, after issuing some output.
So, it may be that the KBD status arrives sooner than for a human
pressing a key. However, it seems that this is rather indeterminate
and so the loop may run indefinitely long. This loop will theoretically
average 120mA through the "0x564" ROM wire for an indefinate length of time.
